          "content": "# Supabase Setup Guide - Step by Step\n\nI'll guide you through setting up the profiles table and trigger on Supabase. Follow these steps carefully.\n\n---\n\n## **Step 1: Access Supabase SQL Editor**\n\n1. Go to [https://supabase.com](https://supabase.com)\n2. Sign in to your account\n3. Click on your **StreamWatch project** (the one with URL: `lnuxpkwnbesqrqsxyiek.supabase.co`)\n4. In the left sidebar, click on **\"SQL Editor\"** icon (looks like `</>`)\n5. Click **\"New query\"** button (top right)\n\n---\n\n## **Step 2: Create the Profiles Table**\n\nCopy and paste this SQL into the editor:\n\n```sql\n-- Create profiles table\nCREATE TABLE profiles (\n  id UUID REFERENCES auth.users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE PRIMARY KEY,\n  username TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n  created_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT NOW(),\n  updated_at TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT NOW()\n);\n\n-- Add comment to table\nCOMMENT ON TABLE profiles IS 'User profile information';\n```\n\n**What this does:**\n- Creates a `profiles` table\n- `id` links to the auth user (UUID)\n- `username` must be unique and cannot be null\n- `ON DELETE CASCADE` means if auth user is deleted, profile is deleted too\n- Timestamps track when profile was created/updated\n\n**Execute:**\n- Click **\"Run\"** button (or press `Ctrl+Enter` / `Cmd+Enter`)\n- You should see: ✅ **\"Success. No rows returned\"**\n\n---\n\n## **Step 3: Enable Row Level Security (RLS)**\n\nCopy and paste this SQL:\n\n```sql\n-- Enable RLS on profiles table\nALTER TABLE profiles ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\n```\n\n**What this does:**\n- Enables security so users can't access data without proper policies\n\n**Execute:**\n- Click **\"Run\"**\n- You should see: ✅ **\"Success. No rows returned\"**\n\n---\n\n## **Step 4: Create RLS Policies**\n\nCopy and paste this SQL:\n\n```sql\n-- Policy 1: Everyone can view all profiles (for displaying usernames)\nCREATE POLICY \"Profiles are viewable by everyone\"\n  ON profiles\n  FOR SELECT\n  USING (true);\n\n-- Policy 2: Users can insert their own profile\nCREATE POLICY \"Users can insert own profile\"\n  ON profiles\n  FOR INSERT\n  WITH CHECK (auth.uid() = id);\n\n-- Policy 3: Users can update their own profile\nCREATE POLICY \"Users can update own profile\"\n  ON profiles\n  FOR UPDATE\n  USING (auth.uid() = id)\n  WITH CHECK (auth.uid() = id);\n\n-- Policy 4: Users can delete their own profile\nCREATE POLICY \"Users can delete own profile\"\n  ON profiles\n  FOR DELETE\n  USING (auth.uid() = id);\n```\n\n**What this does:**\n- **SELECT**: Anyone can read profiles (needed to show usernames in app)\n- **INSERT**: Users can only create their own profile\n- **UPDATE**: Users can only update their own profile\n- **DELETE**: Users can only delete their own profile\n\n**Execute:**\n- Click **\"Run\"**\n- You should see: ✅ **\"Success. No rows returned\"**\n\n---\n\n## **Step 5: Create Auto-Profile Function**\n\nCopy and paste this SQL:\n\n```sql\n-- Function to automatically create profile when user signs up\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.handle_new_user()\nRETURNS TRIGGER\nLANGUAGE plpgsql\nSECURITY DEFINER\nSET search_path = public\nAS $$\nBEGIN\n  INSERT INTO public.profiles (id, username)\n  VALUES (\n    NEW.id,\n    COALESCE(\n      NEW.raw_user_meta_data->>'username',\n      'user_' || substr(NEW.id::text, 1, 8)\n    )\n  );\n  RETURN NEW;\nEXCEPTION\n  WHEN unique_violation THEN\n    -- If username already exists, append random suffix\n    INSERT INTO public.profiles (id, username)\n    VALUES (\n      NEW.id,\n      COALESCE(\n        NEW.raw_user_meta_data->>'username',\n        'user_' || substr(NEW.id::text, 1, 8)\n      ) || '_' || substr(md5(random()::text), 1, 4)\n    );\n    RETURN NEW;\nEND;\n$$;\n```\n\n**What this does:**\n- Automatically creates a profile when a new user signs up\n- Uses username from signup metadata if provided\n- Falls back to `user_12345678` format if no username\n- Handles duplicate usernames by adding random suffix\n\n**Execute:**\n- Click **\"Run\"**\n- You should see: ✅ **\"Success. No rows returned\"**\n\n---\n\n## **Step 6: Create Trigger**\n\nCopy and paste this SQL:\n\n```sql\n-- Trigger that runs the function after user creation\nCREATE TRIGGER on_auth_user_created\n  AFTER INSERT ON auth.users\n  FOR EACH ROW\n  EXECUTE FUNCTION public.handle_new_user();\n```\n\n**What this does:**\n- Automatically runs `handle_new_user()` function whenever a new user is created in `auth.users`\n\n**Execute:**\n- Click **\"Run\"**\n- You should see: ✅ **\"Success. No rows returned\"**\n\n---\n\n## **Step 7: Create Index for Performance**\n\nCopy and paste this SQL:\n\n```sql\n-- Create index on username for fast lookups\nCREATE INDEX idx_profiles_username ON profiles(username);\n```\n\n**What this does:**\n- Makes username searches fast (important if you add username search later)\n\n**Execute:**\n- Click **\"Run\"**\n- You should see: ✅ **\"Success. No rows returned\"**\n\n---\n\n## **Step 8: Verify Setup**\n\nLet's verify everything was created correctly:\n\n```sql\n-- Check if profiles table exists\nSELECT table_name \nFROM information_schema.tables \nWHERE table_schema = 'public' \nAND table_name = 'profiles';\n\n-- Check if trigger exists\nSELECT trigger_name \nFROM information_schema.triggers \nWHERE trigger_name = 'on_auth_user_created';\n\n-- Check if function exists\nSELECT routine_name \nFROM information_schema.routines \nWHERE routine_schema = 'public' \nAND routine_name = 'handle_new_user';\n```\n\n**Execute:**\n- Click **\"Run\"**\n- You should see 3 rows returned:\n  - `profiles` (table)\n  - `on_auth_user_created` (trigger)\n  - `handle_new_user` (function)\n\n---\n\n## **Step 9: Configure Email Templates (Password Reset)**\n\n1. In left sidebar, click **\"Authentication\"**\n2. Click **\"Email Templates\"** tab\n3. Find **\"Reset Password\"** template\n4. Click **\"Edit\"**\n5. Update the **Confirmation URL** to:\n   ```\n   {{ .SiteURL }}/auth/reset-password?token={{ .Token }}&type=recovery\n   ```\n6. Click **\"Save\"**\n\n**What this does:**\n- Configures the password reset email link format\n- We'll handle this deep link in the app\n\n---\n\n## **Step 10: Configure Deep Link Settings**\n\n1. Still in **\"Authentication\"** section\n2. Click **\"URL Configuration\"** tab\n3. Under **\"Redirect URLs\"**, add:\n   ```\n   streamwatch://reset-password\n   streamwatch://auth/callback\n   ```\n4. Click **\"Save\"**\n\n**What this does:**\n- Allows Supabase to redirect to your app after password reset\n- Required for deep linking to work\n\n---\n\n## **Step 11: Test the Setup (Optional)**\n\nLet's create a test profile manually to verify:\n\n```sql\n-- Get your current user ID (if you're logged in)\nSELECT id, email FROM auth.users LIMIT 1;\n\n-- Insert a test profile (replace YOUR_USER_ID with actual ID from above)\nINSERT INTO profiles (id, username)\nVALUES ('YOUR_USER_ID', 'testuser123');\n\n-- Verify it was created\nSELECT * FROM profiles;\n```\n\n**Execute each query separately**\n\nIf successful, you should see your test profile!\n\n---\n\n## **Step 12: Clean Up Test Data (Optional)**\n\nIf you created test data:\n\n```sql\n-- Delete test profile\nDELETE FROM profiles WHERE username = 'testuser123';\n```\n\n---\n\n## **Verification Checklist**\n\nBefore moving to code, verify:\n\n- ✅ `profiles` table exists in **\"Table Editor\"** (left sidebar)\n- ✅ RLS is enabled (you'll see a shield icon next to table name)\n- ✅ 4 policies exist (click on table → \"Policies\" tab)\n- ✅ Trigger `on_auth_user_created` exists\n- ✅ Function `handle_new_user` exists\n- ✅ Email template updated\n- ✅ Redirect URLs configured\n\n---\n\n## **Common Issues & Solutions**\n\n### Issue: \"permission denied for schema auth\"\n**Solution:** Make sure you're using the SQL Editor as the project owner, not a restricted user.\n\n### Issue: \"relation 'profiles' already exists\"\n**Solution:** Table already created. Skip Step 2, continue with Step 3.\n\n### Issue: \"function handle_new_user already exists\"\n**Solution:** Use `CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION` (already in the SQL above).\n\n### Issue: Trigger not firing\n**Solution:** \n```sql\n-- Check if trigger is enabled\nSELECT * FROM pg_trigger WHERE tgname = 'on_auth_user_created';\n```\n\n---\n\n## **Next Steps**\n\nOnce you've completed all steps and verified everything:\n\n1. **Reply with**: \"Database setup complete\" \n2. I'll provide the **mobile app code changes** for:\n   - Username field in signup/signin\n   - Forgot password screen\n   - Password reset screen\n   - Deep link handling\n   - Profile fetching\n\n---\n\n**Take your time with each step. Let me know if you encounter any errors or need clarification on any step!**"
